        <div id="lithium-vs-agm" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Lithium vs AGM: The Real Cost Per Cycle
          </h2>

          <p>
            The sticker price makes lithium look expensive. A 12V 100Ah AGM battery costs roughly a third to half as much as a budget 100Ah LiFePO4 battery, and a fraction of a Battle Born. But the label on an AGM battery overstates what you can use. Lead-acid batteries should not be routinely discharged below about 50%, so a 100Ah AGM gives you about 50Ah, or 600Wh, of practical capacity. A 100Ah LiFePO4 battery can be run to 90% or more without harm, which gives you roughly 1,150Wh from the same rating.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cycle life widens the gap. A good AGM battery cycled to 50% depth of discharge typically delivers somewhere in the range of 400 to 800 cycles before its capacity fades badly. Budget LiFePO4 batteries are commonly rated for 3,000 to 4,000 cycles at 80% to 100% depth of discharge, and premium brands claim more. Even if you discount those ratings heavily, a lithium battery used every weekend will outlast several AGM batteries.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Battery</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Usable energy per cycle</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical rated cycles</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Lifetime energy</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Weight</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">100Ah AGM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 600Wh (50% DoD)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">400-800</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">240-480kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">60-70 lb</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">100Ah LiFePO4 (budget)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1,150Wh (90% DoD)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3,000-4,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3,400kWh+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">22-28 lb</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">100Ah LiFePO4 (premium)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1,150Wh (90% DoD)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3,000-5,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3,400kWh+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">27-31 lb</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Do the arithmetic and the cost per usable kilowatt-hour falls by a factor of five to ten. If an AGM battery delivers 360kWh over its life, every kilowatt-hour costs somewhere around half a dollar or more. A budget LiFePO4 battery that delivers 3,400kWh costs pennies per kilowatt-hour. Even a premium lithium battery comes out far cheaper per cycle, and that is before counting the fuel and generator hours a bigger usable bank saves you.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are two situations where AGM still makes sense. One is a trailer that sits in storage almost all year and only needs to run the breakaway brake and a few lights. The other is a rig that is regularly stored or charged in deep cold with no way to warm the battery. For everyone who actually boondocks, the extra 35 to 40 pounds of weight savings per battery and the doubled usable capacity are reason enough to switch. Our explainer on <a href="/lifepo4-vs-lithium-ion-power-stations/">LiFePO4 versus lithium-ion chemistry</a> covers why LiFePO4 is the safer of the lithium options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How to Size an RV Battery Bank
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with a daily energy budget, not a battery count. List everything that runs on 12V when you are off-grid, estimate how many amp-hours each item uses in a day, and add them up. RV loads are smaller than most people expect until a furnace, an inverter or a residential fridge enters the picture, and then they climb quickly.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Load</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical draw</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Daily use</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">LED lights</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2A total</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-10Ah</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water pump</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-6A while running</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-5Ah</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Furnace blower (cold night)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-5A while running</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25-50Ah</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">12V compressor fridge</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-5A while running</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25-50Ah</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Parasitic draws (detectors, stereo memory)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.3-1A</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7-25Ah</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Laptop and phones via inverter</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-10A</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-30Ah</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Starlink via inverter</td>
