                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30-80Ah if left on</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            A couple running lights, a pump, phones and a 12V fridge in mild weather usually lands between 50 and 90Ah a day. Add a furnace on a cold night and you can double that. Running a residential refrigerator or Starlink all day on an inverter pushes many rigs past 150Ah. Write your own number down, because it decides everything that follows.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, multiply by the number of days you want to go without charging, then divide by about 0.9 to stay off the very bottom of the battery. A 75Ah daily load for two cloudy days works out to roughly 165Ah, so two 100Ah batteries is the right bank. That is the most common setup for weekend boondockers. Full-timers who run a furnace, Starlink and a coffee maker usually end up at 300 to 400Ah plus a meaningful solar array, because no reasonable bank survives a week of heavy use without charging.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rule-of-Thumb Bank Sizes</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">100Ah</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Weekend, lights and pump</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">200Ah</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical boondocking couple</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">300Ah</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Furnace or Starlink use</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">400Ah+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Full-time off-grid</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Do not forget the inverter. Each 100Ah battery with a 100A BMS supports about 1,200W continuous at 12V, after losses. A 2,000W inverter running a microwave can pull more than 170A, so it needs at least two batteries in parallel to stay under the BMS limits. If you want to run an air conditioner, you are looking at a large bank, a big inverter and a soft starter, and for most owners the honest answer is a generator. Our guide to the <a href="/best-rv-generator/">best RV generators</a> covers the models quiet enough for campgrounds.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cold-weather" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Low-Temperature Charging and Self-Heating Batteries
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the one rule that matters most with lithium: LiFePO4 cells must not be charged below freezing, 32F or 0C. Charging cold cells causes lithium plating on the anode, which permanently reduces capacity and in severe cases can create internal shorts. Discharging in the cold is fine down to roughly -4F for most batteries, so the furnace can run all night. The problem is the next morning, when the solar panels start pushing current into frozen cells.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every battery on this list has a BMS that blocks charging below freezing. That protects the battery, but it also means a cold battery simply will not recharge until it warms up. In a tongue-mounted box on a 20F morning, that can take most of the day. Self-heating batteries fix this by using incoming charge current to power internal heating pads first, then accepting charge once the cells are warm enough. Most only heat when a charger, solar controller or DC-DC charger is connected, so they will not drain themselves heating overnight.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">HONEST CAVEAT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A heater warms the cells, not the whole battery box.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Self-heating batteries use a modest amount of power to bring the cells just above freezing. In sustained single-digit temperatures with weak winter sun, the heater can consume a large share of what a small solar array produces. If you camp in real cold, moving the batteries inside the heated envelope of the RV is the better fix.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            So who needs a heater? If you only camp from late spring to early fall, you do not, and a standard battery with low-temperature cut-off is fine. If you camp in the shoulder seasons, or you store a trailer with solar keeping the battery topped up through the winter, a heated battery prevents months of blocked charging. If you camp in true winter, the best answer is to mount the batteries inside the coach, under a dinette seat or in a heated basement, where the furnace keeps them warm.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more point: winter storage. A LiFePO4 battery stored cold and disconnected is perfectly happy, ideally at 50% to 70% state of charge with the battery disconnect switch off. What damages lithium is not storage in the cold, it is charging in the cold, and the BMS on every battery here prevents that. Turn off the disconnect to stop parasitic draws from slowly emptying it over the winter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bms-bluetooth" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. BMS Ratings and Bluetooth Monitoring
          </h2>

          <p>
            The battery management system is the circuit board inside every LiFePO4 battery that decides when the battery is allowed to charge and discharge. It balances the four internal cells, stops charging at high voltage, cuts off before over-discharge, blocks charging in the cold, and disconnects on a short circuit. The cells determine how much energy the battery stores. The BMS determines how much current you can pull and how safely the battery behaves when something goes wrong.
          </p>

          <p>
            The number to read is continuous discharge current. Most 12V 100Ah batteries are rated at 100A continuous, which is about 1,200W at 12V. Some, like the Epoch, list 120A, and surge ratings of 200A or more for a few seconds help start motors. If a load exceeds the BMS rating, the battery shuts off to protect itself, and from inside the RV that looks exactly like a dead battery. When in doubt, add a second battery in parallel: two batteries share the load and double the available current.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bluetooth monitoring is more useful with lithium than with lead-acid, because LiFePO4 voltage barely moves between 90% and 20% state of charge. The four-LED monitor panel in most RVs reads voltage and is nearly useless for lithium. A Bluetooth BMS reports actual state of charge, current in and out, temperature and cell voltages on your phone. Cell voltages are the early warning that matters: a cell that drifts away from the others is the first sign a battery needs attention.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your battery has no Bluetooth, or you have a multi-battery bank, a shunt monitor such as a Victron SmartShunt is often the better tool. It sits on the negative cable, counts every amp in and out of the whole bank, and shows one state-of-charge number for everything. Many owners end up with both: battery Bluetooth for cell health, and a shunt for an accurate whole-bank fuel gauge.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="charging" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Converter, Alternator and Solar Compatibility
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dropping a lithium battery into an RV is not always plug and play. The battery will work with almost any charger, but it will not charge fully or efficiently with one designed for lead-acid. LiFePO4 wants an absorption voltage of about 14.2V to 14.6V, then little or no float. Many older RV converters charge at 13.6V or so, which leaves a lithium battery partly charged and takes far longer to get there.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check your converter's label. Many recent units from WFCO and Progressive Dynamics have a lithium mode or a lithium-specific model, and replacing an older converter with a lithium-compatible one is a common and inexpensive upgrade. Avoid any charger with an automatic desulfation or equalization mode, which pushes voltage higher than lithium needs. If you are unsure, the battery maker's support line can tell you whether your converter is compatible.
          </p>

          <p>
            Alternator charging is where many owners get caught. A lithium battery has very low internal resistance and will draw as much current as an alternator can supply, which can overheat a stock alternator during a long drive. A DC-DC charger, such as those from Victron, Renogy or Redarc, limits current to a safe level and charges at the correct lithium voltage, and it is the right solution for any trailer or motorhome charged while driving.
          </p>

          <p>
            Solar is the easiest of the three. Set your charge controller to its lithium or LiFePO4 profile, or program the absorption voltage manually from the battery's spec sheet. A 200W to 400W roof array is common for a 200Ah bank and can refill it on a sunny day. If you would rather start with portable panels you can aim at the sun, our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/">best portable solar panels</a> covers folding options, and our explainer on <a href="/how-to-charge-a-portable-power-station-with-solar/">charging with solar</a> explains voltage and wiring basics that apply to RV batteries too.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="battery-box" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Battery Boxes, Fusing and Installation
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most travel trailers carry their battery in a plastic box on the tongue. That box was designed for lead-acid, which vents hydrogen gas while charging and needs ventilation. LiFePO4 batteries do not vent gas in normal operation, so a battery box for a trailer with lithium is mainly about physical protection: keeping road spray, rain and debris off the terminals, and holding the battery firmly so it cannot shift and chafe cables.
          </p>

          <p>
            Measure before you buy. A 100Ah lithium battery comes in several case sizes, from compact Group 22NF and Group 24 to the larger Group 27 and Group 31, and the box needs to fit the battery plus its terminals and cables. A strap or hold-down bracket is essential; a loose battery on a bouncing tongue is how terminals crack. Choose a box with a lid that fully covers the terminals, especially if the battery is not rated for water resistance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fusing is the step owners skip and should not. A lithium battery can deliver thousands of amps into a short circuit, far more than lead-acid, so put a properly rated fuse close to the positive terminal. A Class T fuse is the conservative choice for large banks; MRBF terminal fuses are common for single batteries. Size cables for your inverter's maximum current, not for the battery's capacity. If you are not comfortable working with DC wiring at these currents, pay an RV technician for an hour.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you add a second battery, connect them in parallel for a 12V system, use equal-length cables, and take the main positive and negative from opposite ends of the bank so both batteries share the load evenly. Use the same model and age of battery, and charge each one fully before connecting them so they start balanced. Mixing a new lithium battery with an old lead-acid battery in the same bank is a mistake; they charge at different voltages and the bank will be miserable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="battery-vs-power-station" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. RV House Battery or Portable Power Station?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A fair question before you rewire anything: should you upgrade the RV house battery at all, or just buy a portable power station? A power station is a LiFePO4 battery with a built-in inverter, charger, solar controller and outlets. It needs no wiring, moves between the RV, the tent and the house, and works the day it arrives. For many owners of small trailers and vans, that is the simpler answer.
          </p>

          <p>
            A house battery wins once you want the RV's own systems to run from it. The furnace blower, water pump, ceiling lights, slide motors and jacks are all wired to the house battery, and a power station cannot easily feed them without adding a converter or rewiring. A house battery also charges automatically from the tow vehicle, the converter and roof solar, so you never think about it. Per watt-hour, 12V batteries are also cheaper than power stations of the same capacity.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many campers end up with both. A lithium house battery runs the RV's 12V systems, and a portable power station handles the coffee maker, a CPAP, or charging at a tent site away from the trailer. If you are leaning toward a portable unit, our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-camping/">best portable power stations for camping</a> ranks the best options by weight, solar input and runtime, and our explainer on whether <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-refrigerator/">a power station can run a refrigerator</a> works through the fridge math.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more consideration is lifespan. A quality LiFePO4 battery outlasts the inverter and electronics inside a typical power station, and when an RV battery's BMS fails, you replace one part rather than a whole unit. That longevity is one reason house batteries make more sense for rigs used every month. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-portable-power-stations-last/">how long power stations last</a> covers what wears out first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Keeping the old lead-acid converter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many older converters charge at about 13.6V, which never fully charges a lithium battery. Some add a desulfation mode that pushes voltage higher than LiFePO4 wants. Check the label, and if your converter has no lithium setting, replace it with a lithium-compatible model. It is one of the cheapest parts of the upgrade.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Charging straight from the alternator</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A lithium battery will pull as much current as an alternator can make, and on a long tow that can overheat a stock alternator. Install a DC-DC charger between the vehicle and the house battery. It limits current, charges at the correct lithium voltage and protects both the alternator and the battery.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the BMS current limit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 100A BMS supports about 1,200W at 12V. Run a 2,000W inverter with a microwave on one battery and the BMS will shut off, which looks exactly like a dead battery. Add a second battery in parallel or choose a smaller inverter so the load never exceeds the rating.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the fuse</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lithium batteries can deliver enormous current into a short circuit, far more than lead-acid. Fit a correctly rated fuse close to the positive terminal, sized for your cable and inverter. Without one, a chafed cable on a bouncing tongue becomes a fire risk instead of a blown fuse.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing batteries in one bank</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pairing a new lithium battery with an old AGM, or two lithium batteries of different brands and ages, leaves them fighting each other. They charge at different voltages and share load unevenly. Build a bank from identical batteries bought together, and fully charge each one before connecting them in parallel.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best RV battery overall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most owners, a 12V 100Ah LiFePO4 battery is the best RV house battery. Battle Born has the longest track record and best support, SOK and Epoch offer heating and Bluetooth for less, and LiTime and Redodo are the value picks. Choose based on how cold you camp and whether you want app monitoring, since capacity is nearly identical.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I just swap my RV's lead-acid battery for lithium?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Physically, yes, but check your charging sources first. Confirm the converter has a lithium setting or replace it, add a DC-DC charger if you charge from the tow vehicle's alternator, and set your solar controller to its LiFePO4 profile. The battery will run your 12V systems immediately; the charging setup decides whether it fills fully.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many lithium batteries do I need for boondocking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most couples who boondock for a weekend with a 12V fridge, lights, a water pump and phones do well with 200Ah, which means two 100Ah batteries. Add a furnace on cold nights or Starlink and 300Ah is more comfortable. Full-timers usually run 400Ah or more with a substantial solar array to recharge it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do LiFePO4 RV batteries work in cold weather?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They discharge fine down to roughly -4F, so the furnace can run all night, but they must not be charged below freezing. The BMS blocks cold charging to protect the cells. A self-heating battery warms itself using charge current before accepting a charge, and mounting batteries inside the heated coach is the best winter solution.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a vented battery box for a lithium battery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Venting is required for lead-acid batteries, which release hydrogen while charging. LiFePO4 batteries do not vent gas in normal operation. A battery box for a trailer still matters for physical protection, keeping water and road debris off the terminals and holding the battery firmly, so use a strap and a lid that covers the terminals.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a heated lithium battery worth the extra money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if you charge in freezing temperatures. If you camp from late spring to early fall, a standard battery with low-temperature cut-off is fine. If you camp in the shoulder seasons or keep solar charging a stored trailer through winter, a heater avoids days of blocked charging. For true winter camping, move the bank inside instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a LiFePO4 RV battery last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most are rated for 3,000 to 5,000 cycles at deep discharge, and calendar life commonly runs 10 years or more with sensible care. A weekend camper uses well under 100 cycles a year, so capacity loss is rarely the limit. In practice, physical damage, a failed BMS or charging below freezing are what end most batteries early.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

